      "notes": "<h2>The Nose (Structure, Function & Smelling Process)</h2>\n<p>The nose is a <b>sensory organ for smell (olfaction)</b>. It detects chemical substances in the air and converts them into electrical signals that the brain interprets as smells.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 1. MAIN PARTS INVOLVED IN SMELL</h3>\n\n<h4> A. Nostrils</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Openings that allow air to enter the nose</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> B. Nasal Cavity</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Air passage inside the nose</li>\n  <li>Contains mucus and olfactory receptors</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> C. Olfactory Epithelium</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Specialized tissue containing smell receptors</li>\n  <li>Detects chemical particles in the air</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> D. Olfactory Nerve</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Carries smell signals to the brain</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> E. Brain (Olfactory area)</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Interprets signals as specific smells</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 2. HOW SMELL WORKS (STEP-BY-STEP PROCESS)</h3>\n\n<pre>\nOdor molecules → Nose → Olfactory receptors → Olfactory nerve → Brain → Smell recognition\n</pre>\n\n<h4> Step 1: Odor enters nose</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Smell particles (chemicals) enter the nostrils with air</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> Step 2: Dissolving in mucus</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Odor molecules dissolve in mucus inside nasal cavity</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> Step 3: Detection by receptors</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Olfactory receptors detect chemical particles</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> Step 4: Signal formation</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Receptors convert smell into electrical nerve impulses</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> Step 5: Signal travels to brain</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Olfactory nerve carries impulses to the brain</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4> Step 6: Smell interpretation</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Brain identifies and recognizes the smell (e.g., perfume, food, smoke)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 3. HOW WE ACTUALLY SMELL</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The nose does NOT “understand” smell directly</li>\n  <li>It only detects chemical particles in air</li>\n  <li>The brain interprets signals as specific smells</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><b>Key idea:</b> Smell perception happens in the brain, not just the nose.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 4. FUNCTION OF EACH PART</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Nostrils:</b> Allow air and odor particles to enter</li>\n  <li><b>Nasal cavity:</b> Warms, filters, and moistens air</li>\n  <li><b>Mucus:</b> Traps odor particles</li>\n  <li><b>Olfactory receptors:</b> Detect chemicals</li>\n  <li><b>Olfactory nerve:</b> Sends signals to brain</li>\n  <li><b>Brain:</b> Identifies smell</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 5. IMPORTANCE OF SMELL</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Helps detect dangerous gases (smoke, chemicals)</li>\n  <li>Enhances taste of food</li>\n  <li>Helps identify spoiled food</li>\n  <li>Triggers memory and emotions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 6. REAL LIFE EXAMPLE OF SMELLING</h3>\n\n<p><b>Smelling perfume:</b></p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Perfume releases odor molecules into air</li>\n  <li>Molecules enter nose through nostrils</li>\n  <li>Dissolve in mucus inside nasal cavity</li>\n  <li>Olfactory receptors detect them</li>\n  <li>Signals are sent through olfactory nerve</li>\n  <li>Brain recognizes the smell as perfume</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 7. COMMON SMELL PROBLEMS</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Cold:</b> Blocks nasal cavity, reducing smell</li>\n  <li><b>Sinus infection:</b> Causes inflammation and reduced smell</li>\n  <li><b>Anosmia:</b> Loss of smell ability</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> SUMMARY</h3>\n\n<pre>\nNose = chemical detector\nOlfactory receptors = smell sensors\nOlfactory nerve = signal carrier\nBrain = smell interpreter\nSmelling = brain process using nose signals\n</pre>\n",
