      "notes": "<h2>Human Reproductive System (Phase 4: Male & Female Organs in Full Detail)</h2>\n<p>The human reproductive system is responsible for producing sex cells (gametes), enabling fertilization, and supporting the development of a new human being.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3>‍ 1. MALE REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM</h3>\n\n<p>The male reproductive system is designed to <b>produce, store, and deliver sperm cells</b>.</p>\n\n<h4> Main Organs:</h4>\n\n<h4>1. Testes</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Produce sperm cells</li>\n  <li>Produce male hormone (testosterone)</li>\n  <li>Located in the scrotum</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>2. Scrotum</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Holds and protects testes</li>\n  <li>Maintains lower temperature for sperm production</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>3. Sperm Duct (Vas deferens)</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Transports sperm from testes to urethra</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4. Seminal Vesicles</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Add nutrient-rich fluid to sperm</li>\n  <li>Provides energy for sperm movement</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>5. Prostate Gland</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Produces fluid that helps sperm move</li>\n  <li>Improves sperm survival</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>6. Urethra</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Tube that carries sperm out of the body</li>\n  <li>Also carries urine (not at the same time)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>7. Penis</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Organ used to deliver sperm into female body</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3>‍ 2. FEMALE REPRODUCTIVE SYSTEM</h3>\n\n<p>The female reproductive system is designed to <b>produce eggs and support development of the baby</b>.</p>\n\n<h4> Main Organs:</h4>\n\n<h4>1. Ovaries</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Produce egg cells (ova)</li>\n  <li>Release hormones (estrogen and progesterone)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>2. Fallopian Tubes (Oviducts)</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Transport egg from ovary to uterus</li>\n  <li>Site of fertilization</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>3. Uterus (Womb)</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Where embryo develops</li>\n  <li>Has thick walls for protection and nourishment</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4. Endometrium</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Inner lining of uterus</li>\n  <li>Supports implantation of embryo</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>5. Cervix</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Lower part of uterus</li>\n  <li>Controls entry into uterus</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>6. Vagina</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Receives sperm during reproduction</li>\n  <li>Birth canal for baby delivery</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 3. HOW THE SYSTEMS WORK TOGETHER</h3>\n\n<pre>\nMale produces sperm → Female produces egg → Fertilization → Zygote → Pregnancy → Baby\n</pre>\n\n<h4> Step-by-step:</h4>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Sperm is produced in testes</li>\n  <li>Egg is released from ovary</li>\n  <li>Sperm travels through reproductive tract</li>\n  <li>Fertilization occurs in fallopian tube</li>\n  <li>Zygote forms and implants in uterus</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 4. KEY FUNCTIONS OF EACH SYSTEM</h3>\n\n<h4>Male system:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Produces sperm</li>\n  <li>Delivers sperm to female body</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Female system:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Produces eggs</li>\n  <li>Supports fertilization</li>\n  <li>Protects and nourishes developing baby</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 5. DIFFERENCES BETWEEN MALE AND FEMALE SYSTEM</h3>\n\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Male</th>\n    <th>Female</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Produces sperm</td>\n    <td>Produces eggs</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Testes</td>\n    <td>Ovaries</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Continuous production</td>\n    <td>Monthly egg release</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Delivers sperm</td>\n    <td>Receives sperm</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> 6. REAL-LIFE EXAMPLE</h3>\n\n<p><b>Human reproduction process:</b></p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Sperm produced in testes</li>\n  <li>Egg released from ovary</li>\n  <li>Sperm meets egg in fallopian tube</li>\n  <li>Fertilization occurs</li>\n  <li>Embryo develops in uterus</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3> SUMMARY</h3>\n\n<pre>\nMale system = sperm production + delivery\nFemale system = egg production + baby development\nFertilization = occurs in fallopian tube\nUterus = development site for baby\n</pre>\n",
